course: dictionary
lesson: 4784
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word hull is a good example. Read the words around it and ask: is this about a ship, a seed, a place, or an action?
explain: «Ships sink hull first (the hull is the bottom part of a ship) below the horizon.» Here hull means the bottom part of a ship, the part that sits in the water. The clues are ships, sink and horizon, so the meaning fits the boat.
explain: «In Hull, Angus's dad stays in bed for days» is different. Hull is a city in England, and the capital H and the words "In Hull" show it is a place name. Capital letters are clues too.
explain: Hull can also be a verb, a doing word: to hull strawberries means to take off the green leafy top. Ask what the word is doing in the sentence, then swap your meaning in to check it makes sense.
fact: A hull is the main body of a ship or boat, the part that sits in the water.
fact: A hull is also the outer covering of some seeds, nuts or fruits, like the green top of a strawberry.
fact: To hull means to remove the hull, as when you hull strawberries before eating them.
fact: Hull is also the name of a city in England, so it starts with a capital letter.
exercise: In «ships sink hull first (the hull is the bottom part of a ship)», what does hull mean? | The city of Hull | A kind of paint | *The bottom part of a ship | The green top of a strawberry
exercise: Mum says, «We need to hull the strawberries before we eat them.» What must you do? | Paint them red | *Take the green top off | Count how many there are | Put them in the fridge
exercise: «In Hull, Marley's mum makes soup out of everything left.» Why do we know Hull is a place? | It is the verb to hull | *It is a city name with a capital letter, after "In" | It is a ship that sails | It is a green top on a fruit
test: «A hole in the hull lets water in.» What is the hull in this sentence? | *The main body of the ship, the part in the water | The top of a strawberry | The sail on the mast | The name of a city
test: What does it mean to hull something? | *To take off its outer covering | To put it into water | To count how many there are | To paint it bright colours
exam: In «In Hull, Marley's mum makes Marley's Magic Soup», what does Hull mean? | The bottom part of a ship | To remove something | *A city in England | The green top of a strawberry
